  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday: An area of low pressure spinning off \nthe coast of Washington and British Columbia will eject a \nshortwave through the Inland Northwest Saturday, bringing \nwidespread winds out of the southwest. Strong winds in \ncombination with continued warm temperatures and dry conditions \nwill result in critical fire weather concerns late Saturday \nmorning through Saturday evening. The highest wind speeds will \nbe found up the Okanogan Valley and across the Central Columbia \nBasin with sustained speeds of 20-30 mph and gusts of 40-45 mph.\nElsewhere, sustained speeds will range from 10-20 mph with \ngusts of 25-40 mph. For most areas, afternoon humidity values \nwill fall into the teens to low 20s. Any new or existing fires \nwill be capable of very rapid fire spread. Smoke models depict \nthe highest concentrations of surface smoke focused north of \nHighway 2 in WA and north of Interstate 90 in Idaho for \nSaturday, with areas further south staying relatively clear. \nHowever, the smoke forecast will be dependent on the activity of\nany new and ongoing fires and thus is subject to change. For \ntemperatures, Saturday's highs will trend 3-8 degrees cooler \nthan Friday's with afternoon temperatures topping out in the \nupper 80s to low 90s. Isolated readings in the upper 90s will \nlinger for the L-C Valley.\n\nSunday through Thursday: Another weaker shortwave is forecast to \nmove through the region Sunday, bringing a second round of breezy \nsouthwest winds for Central WA. Winds on Sunday won't be nearly as \nstrong as Saturday's, but will still be breezy enough to result in \nelevated to isolated critical fire weather concerns. Sustained \nspeeds Sunday will range from 10-15 mph with gusts of 20-25 mph \nthrough the Cascade gaps, up the Okanogan Valley, and across the \nWestern Columbia Basin. Onshore flow into western Washington will \ncontinue through much of next week with weak shortwaves moving \nthrough and diurnal cross-Cascade pressure gradients bringing breezy \nafternoon winds across the Inland Northwest. Highs will be in the \nmid 80s to low 90s, and overnight lows will be in the 50s to low \n60s. /Fewkes",
